WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.250
The release of the Disney Plus series The
Mandalorian has brought with it one of the

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:07.240
most adorable creatures in the entire Star
Wars canon.

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:12.620
We are, of course, talking about Baby Yoda
whose tiny green frame and big floppy ears

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:14.820
have captured the hearts of fans around the
world.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:16.420
"Stop touching things."

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:29.320
There are still plenty of questions surrounding
Baby Yoda, and Lucasfilm is keeping quiet

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:34.060
for now which, obviously, has only driven
Star Wars fans into more of a frenzy.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.160
Just what is the child's significance to the
greater Star Wars universe?

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:40.600
What is its connection with the Yoda we know
and love?

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:42.800
And how powerful is this child?

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:47.980
Well, those questions might remain a mystery
for now, but in a recent interview with Esquire,

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:53.120
two-time Star Wars director J.J. Abrams offered
up a few suggestions as to why the character

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:54.560
exists in the first place.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:55.680
Abrams explained:

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:57.560
"Star Wars is [...] ever-expanding.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:02.400
And the ability to choose a character like
Yoda and say, 'What if we created a baby Yoda?'

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:06.679
— the reason these things are reasonable
to people is because it's not just nostalgia.

00:01:06.679 --> 00:01:11.130
[...] It's taken something that is meaningful,
a story that has deep roots and potency and

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:13.580
resonates with a human heart, a beating heart."

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.200
Abrams went on to suggest that Baby Yoda's
appeal comes from far more than the creature's

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:18.800
cuteness.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:19.320
He said:

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:23.040
"These are the kind of things that, when they
hit, [...] it's not just cute, but it implies

00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:24.049
a story.

00:01:24.049 --> 00:01:25.340
It sparks the imagination.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:29.569
[...] All these things are about the possibility,
potential, and that's the very heart of what

00:01:29.569 --> 00:01:30.569
Star Wars is."

00:01:30.569 --> 00:01:34.560
With a universe like Star Wars, that has grown
so dramatically from the release of the first

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:39.350
film, there's always going to be room for
more more heroes, more villains, more lightsaber

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:43.259
battles, more evil dictatorships, and, of
course, more cuteness.

00:01:43.259 --> 00:01:47.990
Abrams is clearly in favor of expanding the
Star Wars universe as much as possible and

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:51.899
the introduction of another of Yoda's species
is just one way of doing that.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:53.040
Abrams told Esquire:

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:56.780
"I think that the fun of telling stories in
this galaxy is that you get to take things

00:01:56.789 --> 00:02:01.419
that are familiar and you get to adjust them,
augment them, comment on them, continue them.

00:02:01.419 --> 00:02:03.579
It's a world that is looking to be expanded."

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:07.800
Abrams also pointed out that the Star Wars
galaxy has always been bigger than it actually

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:08.300
seemed.

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:08.980
He said:

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:13.160
"The original movies did the most remarkable
thing in referencing the Clone Wars and the

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:17.400
Empire and the Senate, and the Old Wars, and
never showing any of these things.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:21.380
[...] But you always felt that there was a
peripheral life and history and world beyond

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:22.180
what you were seeing."

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:26.500
But Abrams has kept quiet on whether or not
Baby Yoda's story could have an impact on

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:30.140
The Rise of Skywalker or whether the character
itself could even appear.

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:34.780
But regardless of whether or not The Child
shows up in the final chapter of the Skywalker

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:40.100
Saga, one question remains: could Baby Yoda
help establish the foundation for a new era

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:40.940
of Star Wars films?

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:45.960
George Lucas purposely left Yoda's origins
a mystery to Star Wars fans, making him the

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:49.880
only creature in the known galaxy whose species
is not explicitly named.

00:02:50.109 --> 00:02:54.740
We know he isn't the only one of his kind,
because a female of the species, Master Yaddle,

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:58.780
appeared in The Phantom Menace but little
else is known about Yoda and his origins.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:03.240
Having Baby Yoda feature in upcoming Star
Wars films could potentially clear up a few

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:07.959
mysteries about the tiny green Jedi Master,
from his species' origin to their apparent

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:09.540
natural strength with the Force.

00:03:09.689 --> 00:03:14.080
It would also link the new era of Star Wars
to the old, and tie the small-screen universe

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:14.980
to the silver one.

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:19.460
Unfortunately, Abrams hasn't offered up any
theories as to how much information The Mandalorian

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:21.860
might reveal about Yoda's species.

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:25.820
But there's still a few episodes left of the
show to come and who knows how much deeper

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:27.920
the Star Wars galaxy might get?

